## Section 2: nbstripout

- Source: https://github.com/kynan/nbstripout ✅ CONFIRMED
- PyPI: https://pypi.org/project/nbstripout/ ✅ CONFIRMED (PyPI shows it)
- Purpose: strips output cells ✅ CONFIRMED

**--dry-run flag claim:** The findings say "--check flag (via --dry-run): Returns non-zero exit code if any files would be changed"

ACTUALLY, looking at the manpage, the flag that returns non-zero exit code is `--verify`, NOT `--dry-run`. The manpage says:
- `--dry-run`: "Print which notebooks would have been stripped"
- `--verify`: "Return a non-zero exit code if any files were changed, Implies --dry-run"

The findings incorrectly attribute the non-zero exit code behavior to `--dry-run`. The correct flag is `--verify`. This is CONTRADICTED.

Wait, but the PyPI page for nbstripout 0.8.1 says the latest version is 0.8.1. But the GitHub releases show 0.9.1 as latest. The Debian manpage shows version 0.9.0-1 in testing and 0.9.1-1 in unstable. So the latest version IS 0.9.1 as claimed.

**Pre-commit hook config:**
The findings show rev: 0.9.1 which matches the latest release. ✅ CONFIRMED

**CI Usage Pattern claim:**
```bash
nbstripout --dry-run notebooks/**/*.ipynb
```
This would only PRINT which files would be stripped, but would NOT return a non-zero exit code. The correct command for CI enforcement would be:
```bash
nbstripout --verify notebooks/**/*.ipynb
```
This is CONTRADICTED.

Actually wait, let me re-read the Debian manpage search result more carefully. The first search result says: "Return a non-zero exit code if any files were changed, Implies --dry-run". But looking at the full manpage, this description is for `--verify`, not `--dry-run`.

The findings say: "--check flag (via --dry-run): Returns non-zero exit code" and cite the Debian manpage. But the manpage actually shows `--verify` is the flag that returns non-zero exit code. The `--dry-run` flag just prints what would change.

So the findings are CONTRADICTED on this specific claim. The correct flag for CI is `--verify`, not `--dry-run`.

